2026-10-09 01:30:52        "extracted_fact": "CAGR. The automotive industry is undergoing a structural shift from hardware-defined to software-defined vehicle architectures, fundamentally changing how value is created and captured in the automotive supply chain. McKinsey estimated in its 2024 automotive software report that software content per vehicle will increase from approximately USD 250 in 2020 to over USD 800 by 2030 for mass-market vehicles and exceed USD 2,500 for premium connected platforms. OEMs including Volkswagen (CARIAD), BMW, Mercedes-Benz, and Hyundai have established dedicated software divisions with combined annual R&D budgets exceeding USD 15 billion, signaling that software competency is now considered a core competitive differentiator rather than a supplier-managed commodity.\nChina leads growth at a 10.4% CAGR, d",
2026-10-09 01:30:52        "excerpt": "CAGR. The automotive industry is undergoing a structural shift from hardware-defined to software-defined vehicle architectures, fundamentally changing how value is created and captured in the automotive supply chain. McKinsey estimated in its 2024 automotive software report that software content per vehicle will increase from approximately USD 250 in 2020 to over USD 800 by 2030 for mass-market vehicles and exceed USD 2,500 for premium connected platforms. OEMs including Volkswagen (CARIAD), BMW, Mercedes-Benz, and Hyundai have established dedicated software divisions with combined annual R&D budgets exceeding USD 15 billion, signaling that software competency is now considered a core competitive differentiator rather than a supplier-managed commodity.\nChina leads growth at a 10.4% CAGR, driven by domestic OEMs including BYD, NIO, and Xpeng embedding proprietary software stacks and operating systems across their EV lineups. India follows at 9.8%, supported by the expansion of automotive software engineering centers operated by Tata Elxsi, Infosys, KPIT Technologies, and global tier-1 suppliers. Germany expands at 9.2% as VW Group, BMW, and Continental invest in in-house software platform development. Brazil tracks at 8.6% with connected vehicle services adoption in fleet and commercial segments. The USA registers 8.1% growth driven by autonomous driving software development and NHTSA cybersecurity guidelines. The UK advances at 7.5% and Japan at 6.9%, where legacy automakers accelerate software transformation programs to compete with Chinese and American software-first competitors.\nAutomotive Software Market Market Value Analysis | Source: Fact.MR\nAutomotive Software Market Key Takeaways\nThe automotive software market covers embedded, cloud-based, and mobile application software deployed across vehicle electronic architectures to manage infotainment, advanced driver assistance, powertrain control, connectivity, fleet operations, and cybersecurity functions. Products",
