2026-10-09 19:02:29        "extracted_fact": "Software Complexity and Integration Challenges\nVehicle software complexity creates a scaling problem for OEMs and suppliers. A modern vehicle can contain roughly 100 million lines of code, distributed across legacy control units, high-performance computers, supplier software, diagnostics, network stacks, and cloud-connected services. Consolidating compute functions may simplify hardware architecture over time, but the transition itself creates interface, timing, functional-safety, and validation burdens. Delays often arise from integration dependencies rather than from the coding of an individual feature.\nCybersecurity and update compliance raise the cost of entering and remaining in the market. UNECE Regulation No. 155 requires a cybersecurity management system for vehicle type approval,",
2026-10-09 19:02:29        "excerpt": "Software Complexity and Integration Challenges\nVehicle software complexity creates a scaling problem for OEMs and suppliers. A modern vehicle can contain roughly 100 million lines of code, distributed across legacy control units, high-performance computers, supplier software, diagnostics, network stacks, and cloud-connected services. Consolidating compute functions may simplify hardware architecture over time, but the transition itself creates interface, timing, functional-safety, and validation burdens. Delays often arise from integration dependencies rather than from the coding of an individual feature.\nCybersecurity and update compliance raise the cost of entering and remaining in the market. UNECE Regulation No. 155 requires a cybersecurity management system for vehicle type approval, while Regulation No. 156 establishes requirements for software-update management systems. These frameworks make cybersecurity a continuing organizational and engineering obligation: OEMs must maintain evidence that vulnerabilities, software versions, update processes, and supplier responsibilities are governed throughout the vehicle lifecycle.\nThe EU Cyber Resilience Act broadens this compliance pressure beyond vehicle-specific approval requirements. The regulation entered into force on December 10, 2024, with full compliance requirements applying from December 11, 2027. Suppliers that provide digital elements incorporated into automotive systems must therefore plan for vulnerability handling, documentation, and security updates alongside automotive functional-safety obligations. The burden is particularly material for smaller software specialists that need to demonstrate compliance across multiple OEM programs without a large in-house assurance organization.\nComplexity and cybersecurity are changing the commercial model of automotive software development. OEMs can no longer treat integration as a final engineering phase once individual suppliers have delivered their components. Ar",
