2026-10-09 21:48:35        "extracted_fact": "Electric vehicles represented USD 7.6 billion, or 38.5%, in 2025 and are projected to expand at a 15.1% CAGR. Battery-electric vehicles, plug-in hybrids, and fuel-cell vehicles require software for battery state estimation, thermal management, power delivery, charging interaction, and energy optimization. The IEA's outlook for sustained growth in electric-car sales supports this faster trajectory. Software value is concentrated where vehicle efficiency, charging reliability, and component durability depend on continually refined control strategies.\nOn-premises software generated USD 11.3 billion in 2025, representing 57.1% of market revenue, and is forecast to grow at a 12.6% CAGR. OEMs retain on-premises environments for functions involving safety-critical development, proprietary vehicle",
2026-10-09 21:48:35        "excerpt": "Electric vehicles represented USD 7.6 billion, or 38.5%, in 2025 and are projected to expand at a 15.1% CAGR. Battery-electric vehicles, plug-in hybrids, and fuel-cell vehicles require software for battery state estimation, thermal management, power delivery, charging interaction, and energy optimization. The IEA's outlook for sustained growth in electric-car sales supports this faster trajectory. Software value is concentrated where vehicle efficiency, charging reliability, and component durability depend on continually refined control strategies.\nOn-premises software generated USD 11.3 billion in 2025, representing 57.1% of market revenue, and is forecast to grow at a 12.6% CAGR. OEMs retain on-premises environments for functions involving safety-critical development, proprietary vehicle data, hardware-in-the-loop testing, and tightly controlled validation workflows. These systems remain important where latency, IP protection, and certification evidence cannot be fully delegated to external infrastructure.\nCloud-based software accounted for USD 8.5 billion, or 42.9%, in 2025 and is projected to grow at an 8.9% CAGR. Cloud deployment supports fleet-level analytics, remote diagnostics, connected services, and over-the-air campaign management. Its comparatively lower forecast growth in this market reflects the continued importance of embedded and controlled engineering environments, rather than a reduction in cloud relevance. The strongest architectures are likely to combine cloud-enabled fleet operations with locally controlled vehicle-development and safety-validation processes.\nOEM sales represented USD 13.3 billion, or 67.2%, of 2025 market revenue. Direct OEM integration remains the principal route to market because software must be designed into vehicle architecture, tested with hardware, and maintained through type-approval and lifecycle obligations.\nThe aftermarket generated USD 6.5 billion in 2025 and is forecast to grow at a 12.8% CAGR. Its growth is linked",
